{"files":[{"content":"#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\n/* Maximum size (in char) for neighbors and compute lists */\n#define MAX 100\n\ntypedef struct {\n  int id;\n  float value;\n  char neighbors[MAX];\n} Node;\n\n/* Reads input, allocates memory, and returns the graph. */\nNode* read_input(char* fname, int *n, char* list) {\n  FILE *fl;\n  int opened = 0;\n  char line[MAX * 4];\n  Node *data = NULL;\n\n  if (fname == NULL) {\n    fl = stdin;\n  } else {\n    fl = fopen(fname, \"r\");\n    opened = 1;\n  }\n\n  if (fl == NULL) {\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Error!\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n  }\n\n  list[0] = '\\0';\n  if (fgets(line, sizeof(line), fl) == NULL ||\n      sscanf(line, \"%d %99[^\\n]\", n, list) < 1 || *n < 0) {\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Error!\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n  }\n\n  if (*n > 0) {\n    data = (Node*) calloc((size_t) *n, sizeof(Node));\n    if (data == NULL) {\n      fprintf(stderr, \"Error!\\n\");\n      exit(1);\n    }\n  }\n\n  for (int i = 0; i < *n; i++) {\n    if (fgets(line, sizeof(line), fl) == NULL ||\n        sscanf(line, \"%d %f %99[^\\n]\", &data[i].id, &data[i].value,\n               data[i].neighbors) < 2) {\n      fprintf(stderr, \"Error!\\n\");\n      exit(1);\n    }\n  }\n\n  if (opened)\n    fclose(fl);\n\n  return data;\n}\n\n/* Returns the index of the first node with the given id, or -1 if absent. */\nstatic int find_by_id(Node g[], int id, int size) {\n  int i = 0;\n  while (i < size && g[i].id != id)\n    i++;\n  return i < size ? i : -1;\n}\n\n/* Selects the same successor as the original recursive computation. */\nstatic int select_successor(Node *g, int nd, int size) {\n  char aux[MAX];\n  char *saveptr = NULL;\n  char *token;\n  int best_node = -1;\n  float max = 0.0f;\n\n  strcpy(aux, g[nd].neighbors);\n  token = strtok_r(aux, \" \", &saveptr);\n  while (token != NULL) {\n    int d = find_by_id(g, atoi(token), size);\n    if (d >= 0 && max < g[d].value) {\n      max = g[d].value;\n      best_node = d;\n    }\n    token = strtok_r(NULL, \" \", &saveptr);\n  }\n\n  return best_node;\n}\n\n/* Recursive fallback with the reference computation's evaluation order. */\nstatic float compute_recursive(Node *g, int nd, int size) {\n  int next = select_successor(g, nd, size);\n  if (next < 0)\n    return g[nd].value;\n  return g[nd].value + compute_recursive(g, next, size);\n}\n\n/* Walk the selected chain, then accumulate from its terminal node upward. */\nstatic float compute_iterative(Node *g, int nd, int size, int *path) {\n  int path_len = 0;\n  int current = nd;\n\n  while (current >= 0 && path_len < size) {\n    path[path_len++] = current;\n    current = select_successor(g, current, size);\n  }\n\n  if (path_len == 0)\n    return 0.0f;\n\n  float value = g[path[--path_len]].value;\n  while (path_len > 0)\n    value = g[path[--path_len]].value + value;\n\n  return value;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n  char *fileN = NULL;\n  char computelist[MAX] = {0};\n  char *query_text[MAX];\n  int query_node[MAX];\n  float result[MAX];\n  int query_count = 0;\n  int n = -1;\n  Node *input = read_input(fileN, &n, computelist);\n\n  for (char *c = strtok(computelist, \" \"); c != NULL;\n       c = strtok(NULL, \" \")) {\n    query_text[query_count] = c;\n    query_node[query_count] = find_by_id(input, atoi(c), n);\n    query_count++;\n  }\n\n  /* Use per-thread scratch space only when there is enough work to share. */\n  if (n >= 64 && query_count >= 4) {\n    #pragma omp parallel\n    {\n      int *path = (int*) malloc((size_t) n * sizeof(int));\n\n      #pragma omp for schedule(dynamic, 1)\n      for (int i = 0; i < query_count; i++) {\n        if (query_node[i] < 0)\n          result[i] = 0.0f;\n        else if (path != NULL)\n          result[i] = compute_iterative(input, query_node[i], n, path);\n        else\n          result[i] = compute_recursive(input, query_node[i], n);\n      }\n\n      free(path);\n    }\n  } else {\n    int *path = n > 0 ? (int*) malloc((size_t) n * sizeof(int)) : NULL;\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < query_count; i++) {\n      if (query_node[i] < 0)\n        result[i] = 0.0f;\n      else if (path != NULL)\n        result[i] = compute_iterative(input, query_node[i], n, path);\n      else\n        result[i] = compute_recursive(input, query_node[i], n);\n    }\n\n    free(path);\n  }\n\n  for (int i = 0; i < query_count; i++)\n    printf(\"%s: %f\\n\", query_text[i], result[i]);\n\n  free(input);\n  return 0;\n}\n","path":"src/graph.c"}],"hypothesis":"For sufficiently large graphs with several requested nodes, dynamic OpenMP work sharing across independent iterative path evaluations can reduce runtime while per-thread scratch storage avoids recursive stack growth; smaller workloads stay serial to limit parallel overhead.","notes":"The selected successor still uses the original neighbor order and strict comparison against zero. Each path is accumulated from its terminal node toward the request, preserving the recursive addition order, and results are buffered to retain output order. No execution or measurement was performed.","research":{"queries":[],"urls":[]},"sources_kept":[],"transformations":["Evaluate each requested node by iteratively walking its selected successor chain and accumulating values in reverse path order.","Use dynamic OpenMP scheduling with one-request chunks only when both graph size and request count exceed general granularity thresholds; keep smaller workloads serial." ]}